	<ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="tb-00" contextRef="c-00">
&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; font-variant: normal; margin: 0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 1 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Company Structure and Nature of Operations&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USG Pipeline Company, LLC ("USGPC") is a wholly owned subsidiary of USG Corporation. USGPC converted from a corporation to an LLC in 2011.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC operates approximately 15 miles of 10" interstate pipeline, which was built to transport natural gas from a single interconnection with an interstate pipeline in Marion County, Tennessee to Jackson County, Alabama. USGPC only provides transportation services. It has no storage, compression, gathering or production facilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC was initially certificated, in 1997, in order to serve a new gypsum wallboard plant which was constructed by its affiliate, United States Gypsum Company ("USGypsum"). Although it has offered open-access services to the public since 1999, USGPC performed transportation service for only USGypsum prior to 2012. It did so pursuant to a Part 157 transportation agreement contained in Volume 2 of its tariff, which prescribed charges different from USGPC's open access rates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;Since early 2012, USGPC has provided Firm Transportation ("FT") and Interruptible Transportation ("IT") transportation service pursuant to its open access transportation tariff. In early 2012, USGPC added a delivery point (also in Jackson County, Alabama) for a new customer, The Cooperative District for Northeast Alabama Gas ("CDNAG"), which agreed to reimburse USGPC for costs incurred in connection with the new CDNAG delivery point. To address this operational change, USGPC modified its receipt point and also added a meter at its delivery point to its affiliate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;In order to free up firm capacity for CDNAG, USGypsum converted from its Part 157 service to an open access transportation agreement in February 2012, whereupon USGPC eliminated Volume 2 of its tariff. USGypsum executed FT and IT agreements at the maximum rates. CDNAG executed a long-term firm agreement at a negotiated rate and an IT agreement at the maximum rate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC is operated by personnel of its affiliate USGypsum and its parent, USG Corporation. USGPC has no dedicated personnel of its own. USGPC has a cash management agreement with USG Corporation, which was filed with FERC in 2012.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 2 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Summary of Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Basis of Accounting &lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC's accounting is subject to the requirements of the FERC. Since 2012, USGPC has maintained its accounts according to the Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America. Prior to that time, USGPC had a waiver of the Uniform System of Accounts and, except for a limited Form 2 (page 520) for 2011; it was not obligated to prepare and file a Form 2 for any period prior to 2012. There are no significant differences between regulatory accounting treatment and accounting treatment under accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America (GAAP) presented in these statements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;The preparation of financial statements in accordance with the FERC accounting requirements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the amounts reported in the financial statements and accompanying notes. These estimates include, but are not limited to, long-lived asset impairment, the effects of regulation, the effects of contingencies, etc. The estimates and assumptions used are based upon management's evaluation of the relevant facts and circumstances as of the date of the financial statements. Actual results could differ from those estimates. On an ongoing basis, management reviews estimates based on currently available information. Changes in facts and circumstances may result in revised estimates&lt;b&gt;.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Cash Management&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC has a cash management agreement with USG Corporation, which was filed with the FERC in 2012. The agreement establishes the rights and obligations under which USGPC will advance available, net cash funds resulting from its business activities to USG Corporation, either directly or through a wholly-owned affiliate(s) as may be designated by either party from time to time to perform actions consistent with the cash management agreement, and USG Corporation will provide cash management services for all funds so advanced.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;Property, plant and equipment is recorded at original cost.&amp;#xa0; Depreciation and amortization expense is calculated using a straight-line method.&amp;#xa0; Since it commenced open-access transportation services in 2012, USGPC has used a 5.0% depreciation rate for all of its facilities consistent with its applicable open-access rates.&amp;#xa0; Property, plant and equipment is tested for impairment whenever events or circumstances indicate that its carrying value may not be recoverable.&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC recognizes at fair value legal obligations associated with the retirement of tangible long-lived assets that result from the acquisition, construction or development, and/or normal operation of the assets. A liability is recorded for these obligations as long as the fair value can be reasonably estimated, even if the timing or method of settling the obligation is unknown. The asset retirement obligations are accreted using a credit-adjusted risk-free interest rate commensurate with the expected settlement dates of the asset retirement obligations. This rate is determined at the date the obligation is incurred. The associated retirement costs are capitalized as part of the related long-lived assets and are depreciated over the useful lives of the assets. Subsequent changes resulting from revisions to the timing or the amount of the original estimate of undiscounted cash flows are recognized as an increase or decrease in the carrying amount of the liability and the associated retirement cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC's asset retirement obligations include reclamation requirements as regulated by government authorities related to USGPC's natural gas pipeline. The accounting for asset retirement obligations requires estimates by management about the timing of asset retirements, the cost of retirement obligations, discount and inflation rates used in determining fair values and the methods of remediation associated with its asset retirement obligations. USGPC uses assumptions and estimates that reflect the most likely remediation method. USGPC's estimated liability for asset retirement obligations is revised whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate that a revision to the estimate is necessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Operating Expenses&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC has no dedicated personnel of its own and is operated by personnel of its affiliate, USGypsum, and its parent, USG Corporation. Operating expenses consist primarily of personnel and overhead costs from those entities and costs for outside professional services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC transports natural gas for shippers under a tariff regulated by the FERC. The tariff specifies the general terms and conditions of transportation service on the natural gas pipeline system. Our revenues are derived from agreements for the receipt and delivery of natural gas at points along the pipeline system as specified in each shipper's individual transportation contract. USGPC does not own the natural gas that it transports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;Revenue is recognized in the month in which the contractual performance obligations are satisfied, which is during the month in which the transportation services are provided. USGPC's contracts are on an interruptible basis and USGPC does not have an obligation to perform the transportation service until the customer's request has been accepted. Revenue for these transportation services is fee-based, valued based on the rate in effect for the month of service per quantity that the customer choses to and that USGPC accepted. Revenue is recognized in the month of service based on the volumes of natural gas USGPC transports. Payment is generally received from the customer in the month following performance of the services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 3 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Income Taxes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;As a limited liability company, USGPC does not directly pay federal or state income taxes. USGPC's taxable income or loss is included in the federal income tax returns of the single member. However, since federal and state income taxes are a cost component in rates allowed by the FERC, these taxes are included in the financial statements. This accounting treatment represents a difference from how USGPC would account for such transactions under accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America (GAAP).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;Income taxes are accrued in accordance with the FERC Policy Statement on Income Tax Allowance permitting the accrual of income taxes on a pass-through entity. Deferred income taxes are recognized for difference between the financial reporting and tax bases of assets and liabilities at enacted statutory tax rates in effect for the years in which the differences are expected to reverse. Actual income taxes could vary from these estimates due to future changes in income tax law or results from the final review of tax returns by federal or state tax authorities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USG Corporation and its consolidated subsidiaries are party to an agreement (Tax Sharing Agreement) that provides for the allocation of consolidated tax liabilities and benefits. The Tax Sharing Agreement generally provides that each party is allocated an amount of tax similar to that which would be owed had the party been separately subject to tax. Any net benefit attributable to the parent is reallocated to other members. USGPC is a disregarded entity for U.S. federal income tax purposes and the results of its operations are reported as a component of USG Corporation. There is no Tax Sharing Agreement that governs the allocation of cash payments and receipts between USG Corporation and its disregarded group members, any difference between the cash flows paid or received and the allocation method used for financial reporting purposes is reported in the separate financial statements of USGPC as a contribution to the capital of the party receiving the benefit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC recorded a benefit of ($99,356) and ($95,332) to income tax expense (benefit) and corresponding dividend during the quarters ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, to reflect the effects of intercompany tax sharing that will not be settled via a Tax Sharing Agreement. The cumulative impact to retained earnings and corresponding dividends to reflect intercompany tax allocations that will not be settled via a Tax Sharing Agreement total $4,992,524 and $4,510,880 as of June 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 4 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Legal Proceedings&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;In the normal course of its business, USGPC may be named as defendant in various legal proceedings. USGPC accrues a liability related to such matters when an obligation becomes probable and can be estimated. Management is not aware of any legal matters that would have a material impact on USGPC's financial position, results of operations, or cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Environmental Matters&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;The operations of USGPC are subject to extensive and evolving federal, state and local environmental laws and regulations intended to protect the public health and the environment. Such environmental laws and regulations affect operations as they relate to impacts on air, water and land. USGPC had no liabilities required to be recorded for such matters as of June 30, 2026 or 2025.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;
</ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
</xbrl>